<%# One category and the rules that fill it. Locals: `group` (BudgetPagePresenter::Group) and
    `groups` (the whole fill order — the card is a row of that drag list).

    THE LIST IS THE PAGE, WHERE IT USED TO BE ONE ACCOUNT'S BAND (two-ledger spec §2). Priority was
    compared only inside an account while the fill walked `account.child_pools.by_priority`;
    `AllocationCalculator` fills every holder off one root now, so `groups` is the whole order and
    the scope of one reorder.

    The header says how the category is doing in the app's ONE row vocabulary
    (`HomeHelper#pool_status_label`, spec §4.4) — the same words, the same red, as Home and the
    distribution screen. A second vocabulary here would have two screens describe one envelope
    differently on the same afternoon.

    `priority` is stated because this page is where funding priority is set; the number is what
    decides who gets filled first when the money runs out, and dragging this card — or its ▲▼ —
    is what writes it. %>
<section class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded"
         data-category-group="<%= group.category.name %>"
         data-category-id="<%= group.category.id %>"
         data-app--budget-page--reorder-target="row"
         draggable="true"
         data-action="dragstart->app--budget-page--reorder#start
                      dragend->app--budget-page--reorder#finish">
  <%# STACKS BELOW `sm` (design review H5). Four things wanted this row — two 44px arrows, the
      category's name, its priority and a status clause that runs to "$400.00 left · last period" —
      and at 375px the status wrapped under the arrows and the name truncated to a syllable. The
      handle and the name keep the first line; the status takes the second, indented past the
      arrows so it still reads as belonging to this category.

      `items-center` and not `items-baseline`, because the arrows are now 44px tall (M4) and a
      baseline-aligned 44px control drags the whole row's text to its bottom edge. %>
  <div class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-200 flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:justify-between sm:items-center gap-1 sm:gap-4">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-3 min-w-0">
      <%= render "reorder_controls", group: group, groups: groups %>
      <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 truncate"><%= group.category.name %></h3>
      <span class="text-xs text-gray-500 shrink-0">priority <%= group.priority %></span>
    </div>
    <%# BOTH SUFFIXES, and neither is optional here — which is a property of the SHAPE rather
        than of this file remembering to pass them (see `shared/_holding_status`).

        `period_closed:` says WHICH PERIOD the figure belongs to, and a swept rate-rule envelope
        reading `$400.00 left · last period` on one screen while another reads `$400.00 left` for
        the same category on the same afternoon is two screens describing one envelope
        differently. It rides off the status's own memoised calculator, so this page still builds
        exactly one calculator per category.

        `changed_after_distributing:` is the same objection said about the other clause, and this
        page is the one that owed it most: the row below is the very form the user changed — the
        clause names the act, on the screen the act happened on. Through the group, never a query
        of the partial's own: the period it compares against is `today`'s. Silent on every state
        but `:behind` (HomeHelper#pool_status_label owns that gate).

        Passing an OBJECT rather than two keywords is what makes an omission unwritable rather
        than merely absent. `· holds $X` rides on `Group#balance_clause?`. %>
    <%= render "shared/holding_status", row: group %>
  </div>

  <% group.rules.each do |rule| %>
    <%= render "rule_row", rule: rule %>
  <% end %>
</section>
